   144	## 2. Writing rules. Every rule of engagement stated (IN-15); every graded rule has one reading (IN-16), from a sentence or from determinate evidence (0b).
   145	- W1. Never explain the trap. No "not only X", no "note that", no trap lists, no rule counts, no sentence explaining why a carve-out exists or restating the consequence of a definition. QC fails the same act as IN-17 (01:47) in instruction.md. Our W1 stays stricter than IN-17: counts and trap lists are banned in every agent-visible file. [v3: kept; a data-side trap list passed QC in 4e2f6925 and gave no gain, g4/C_4e2f6925.md:10, :17; CONFLICTS C6]
   146	- W2. Put each rule in the file that governs its data, not next to where it is used. The instruction need not point at the file (01:45 "need not name the file"); a pointer is optional.
   147	- W3. Use the plain forms that worked: a bare property with no example; one clause inside a long enumeration; a definition with a branch and no warning; a count defined by a balancing identity with another file; a global default with the exception in another section.
   148	- W4. An override names the field exactly.
   149	- W5. Stated levers: shipped data includes the separating case for L5 only; for the others the separating cases are in hidden reseeded inputs only, and the shipped data never contradicts them. [v3: S2, g1 GS:87-93] For Shape D this is the design goal: write `WORK/BRANCH_FIRE.md`, one row per stated branch: `| rule | branch | fires on shipped (count) | fires on hidden (seeds) |`; the hard branch has shipped count 0 and fires on every hidden corpus that grades it. L11 inverts this: the shipped input MUST hold one separating witness.
   150	- W6. [v3: CONFLICTS C7, g2/C_37a0c3d6.md:19, :44] A worked example may show one rule on invented toy values that appear in no shipped or hidden file, at most one per rule. It never uses a shipped record or a graded figure and never says which case is the trap. No other worked example in which the right and wrong readings differ, except the single unlabelled L11 witness.
   151	- W7. A shipped control is real and honest, and reconciles under both the right and the wrong reading of every lever.
   152	- W8. A settling rule may sit in an odd visible carrier (a TOML comment, a table footnote, an erratum file) when it is visible and has one reading. When several documents carry rules, one instruction sentence names which document wins (for example "where a file and the standard disagree, the standard wins", 8764bfdd instruction.md:1, g4 GS:66-77).
   153	- W9. Literal-reading self-test: read every rule sentence literally and alone; for L11, read every witness literally and alone. If that reading makes the oracle lose points, rewrite.
   154	- W10. [v3: CONFLICTS C10 and breadth, g2 GS:23, g3 GS:27] One rule register. `docs/` (or the task's equivalent) holds a register file naming every governing file; each rule is one sentence in the file that governs its data (W2). instruction.md states the job, the output paths, whole-document exactness, the rerun promise (G5) and the rules of engagement (work directory, process order, files that may change, exact invocation). It states no rule of the computation. Wholesale delegation ("follow the register") makes every rule it invokes an AL-2 row (05:33).
   155	- W11. No answer for any graded document is visible to the agent (Ayush decision 5): no answer key, golden or sample output, solved list, expected figure, claimed total or "the correct result is" sentence for any graded document, anywhere in `environment/`. Allowed: honest controls per W7, toy worked examples per W6, the single unlabelled L11 witness. An output-shape example (FC:492) carries placeholder or out-of-scope values.
   156	- [v3: g1 GS:46-52, 6d12f970] W12. When the generator re-renders rule documents per hidden corpus (dates, ids, thresholds), the rendered sentences stay word-for-word except the values, and the rule register lists which values change.
