    34	- AL-3 Exact-shape agreement: output formats, error-object shapes, key names, exit codes, file paths, precedence orders. The classic bug: instruction demands {"error": "x", "version": N} while the test asserts the object equals {"error": "x"}. Compare every literal shape the agent can see against every shape anything grades, byte by byte. "Can see" means the instruction and anything shipped in the input directories environment/Dockerfile copies into the agent image, whether or not the prompt names them; read those files as part of the contract, because for most bundles the exact shapes live there rather than in the prompt. "Anything grades" means the tests AND any judged criterion that names a shape, since a criterion demanding one format while the contract states another traps the agent exactly as a test would. Any mismatch where following the contract loses reward is an automatic fail, and a shape that contradicts between the instruction and a shipped spec file is the same fail.
    35	- AL-4 Hidden behaviours: a behaviour of the DELIVERABLE that the tests, an outcome judged criterion, or the solution handles while the contract forbids or omits it, where the contract is the instruction together with whatever the copied input establishes (AL-1) (extra error strings when the contract says "do not invent new error strings", undocumented edge cases carrying graded weight in either layer). Fail. Conduct criteria are NOT hidden behaviours and never belong in this finding: JD-15 exempts them from needing a sentence anywhere, so a criterion about verification habits or honest reporting being absent from the contract is correct by design, not a defect.
    36	- AL-5 The oracle passes its own tests: trace solve.sh output through every test. Any test the oracle would fail proves the bundle broken. Fail.
    37	- AL-6 The judged criteria agree with the solution and the instruction (JD-7/JD-10 deep pass): re-verify the three most load-bearing criteria yourself.
    38	- AL-27 Trigger depth: a rule only counts as graded when the test's input actually constructs the situation where the rule matters. A test can be named after a rule, tick the AL-2 mapping on paper, and still never exercise it because no fixture creates the triggering case. The classic shape is an ordering or precedence rule ("check the magic before the version"): unless some graded input carries BOTH faults at once, the order never matters and a solver checking things backwards still scores full reward. For every ordering, precedence, or tie-break rule the bundle grades, whether the instruction states it or the copied input establishes it (AL-1), trace the fixtures: for each adjacent pair in the chain, find the graded input with both conditions present and the assertion that the earlier one wins. For every other rule, ask "which graded input would come out differently if the solver broke exactly this rule?" - no such input means the rule is ungraded regardless of what the test is called. Fail; name the rule and the missing fixture.
    39	- AL-28 No penalty stacking. For every negative check in test_output.py AND every negative-weight criterion in judge.toml, name the single concrete agent action that triggers it (hardcoded the shipped answers; edited a file outside the allowed scope; read tests/; emitted nondeterministic output...). Group the negatives by triggering action, ACROSS both layers. One mistake must cost at most one maximum-severity penalty. In the tests, sum the absolute weights in each group: a combined penalty above 5 is stacked, the agent punished twice or three times for one decision and the reward floor distorted for everyone. In `tests/judge.toml`, stacking is two negative-weight criteria pricing one act (sum of absolute weights above 5). Across layers it is one act subtracting in the tests and again in a negative-weight criterion. Example shape: the same hardcoding act firing a -5 check and a -3 check costs 8 for one decision, and the same act firing a -5 judged criterion subtracts a third time in the other channel. Fix: keep the strictest single penalty and retarget or drop the rest, or carve the triggers into genuinely non-overlapping conditions so no single action fires more than one. Fail; show the group, its members, and what each one costs.
