    35	- TS-8 No skip switches (`SKIP_TESTS=1`, `if [ -f /app/skip ]`, env-var bypasses).
    36	- TS-9 Never `source`/`exec`/import from agent-writable paths; not PATH-shadowable (does not rely on bare tool names resolvable to /app; absolute paths or controlled PATH).
    37	- TS-10 `tests/reward.toml` combines the channels correctly. It holds exactly one `[[reward]]` table with `name = "reward"`, `aggregation = "weighted-sum"` and `weights` naming exactly `test_output` and `judge`, plus one `[scoring.test_output]` table with `aggregation = "weighted-sum"`, and no other keys or tables. `weights.test_output` equals 0.5 divided by the sum of positive weights registered in test_output.py, and `weights.judge` equals 0.5 divided by the sum of positive criterion weights in judge.toml, both written at full float precision (Python's `repr` of the division). Recompute both from the actual files. The final reward is then half of (test weight earned / test positive total) plus half of (judge weight earned / judge positive total): 1 when every positive passes and no negative fires, 0 for doing nothing, -1 at worst. No clamp exists anywhere and none is needed, because neither channel's negatives may outweigh its positives (TS-23, gate 04 JD-12). A weight left stale after a check or criterion changed is a fail: the score then stops running from -1 to 1. No other top-level `.py` file under tests/ defines criteria, no other `.toml` carries `[judge]` or `[[criterion]]`, and no subdirectory of tests/ holds either, since rewardkit would score each one as an extra input.
