    49	- TS-18 Tolerances, when legitimate (floats), must be stated in the instruction. A hidden tolerance, or a missing one that makes honest float output fail, is a fail.
    50	- TS-19 Every graded behaviour must be learnable by the agent, and every promise the instruction makes must be graded by something. Learnable means stated in the instruction OR determinate in the input the agent is handed, meaning any directory `environment/Dockerfile` copies into the agent image; the prompt may delegate the detail rather than restate it and need not name the file, so read the shipped input before calling anything undocumented. A check grading a rule of engagement rather than a property of the deliverable is the exception: a check on what the agent left alone, or on which paths it touched, needs a sentence that states the rule, because discovery establishes facts and never prohibitions (gate 01 IN-15). On the coverage direction, do not demand that a test cover every promise: a promise may legitimately be graded by a judged criterion instead, which is common for things no assertion can decide. Flag a promise nothing grades at all, never one the judged channel handles. (The full cross-walk happens in Gate 05; flag obvious mismatches you see now, especially error-object shapes and extra undocumented error cases.)
    51	- TS-20 Weights map to importance semantically. The scale means something: 5 = a core outcome, the deliverable's primary behaviour; 3 = an important sub-goal or key edge case; 1 = a minor detail or existence check. Negatives: -5 = a red-line cheat; -3 = a serious secondary violation; -1 = rare, minor. Judge every weight against what the test actually grades: a core-deliverable test carrying weight 1, or an existence/formatting check carrying 5, is wrong. Then check the ORDERING: if the instruction makes clear that property A matters more than property B, but B's test outweighs A's, the reward contradicts the task's own priorities - that is a fail, not a style call. Mild imbalance within the right ordering is a minor. All-identical weights are a fail (script).
    52	- TS-25 Positive tests grade earned work, never an absence. Read every positive test's decisive assertion: if it passes because something bad is missing (an empty violations list, a banned import that is absent, output that "does not contain" a thing), it hands its points to any solver that simply did nothing wrong, and it inflates the positive total so every real test is worth proportionally less. Absence belongs on the negative side with inverted polarity: a `negative_<cheat>` check that returns True when the bad behaviour IS present, registered with a negative weight. A positive-weight check whose pass condition is an absence is a fail; keep the detection logic, move it to a negative check.
