    59	- TS-30 Agent file modes are survivable. The oracle installs its program with `cp`, which leaves mode 0644; a real agent writes it through an editor that renames a temp file into place, so the same program arrives at 0600, owned by uid 1000. Harbor hands `/app` to the verifier with those modes intact, and the verifier runs as root, so reading the file is only a problem once a check drops privileges to run it (`setpriv --reuid=nobody`, `runuser`, or Popen `user=`). Then the unprivileged child cannot open the program, every check that executes it returns False on a perfectly correct submission, and the plain oracle run still reads 1.000, so nothing in QC notices until a real trajectory is graded and loses that whole tier. Any verifier that executes agent-written code under a dropped uid must make it readable first: chmod the program and the directories above it (root may), or run a copy the verifier staged itself. Relaxing or removing the privilege drop is not the fix; that trades this bug for TS-24. tests_qc H6 checks the three shapes statically and fails when it finds none; the proof is the `--owner-only` oracle run in Step 10, which reproduces the agent's 0600 modes and must still read exactly 1.000. A bundle that scores lower there is a fail. The owner's own execute bit survives that run (a file already executable becomes 0700, not 0600), so a check may require an executable artifact, but only when the instruction or an agent-visible spec says to make it executable; requiring a mode or a shebang nothing tells the agent about is a CL-5 hidden convention.
