The external papers reinforce the same constraint: new tasks need generalization pressure, but low scores alone are weak evidence if the test oracle or wording is faulty. Recent audits found defects even in major SWE benchmarks ([Terminal-Bench paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.11868), [SWE-Bench Pro audit](https://openai.com/index/separating-signal-from-noise-coding-evaluations/)). I’m turning this into a source ledger and idea shortlist with explicit fairness and blind-kill checks.
