+VERDICT: Pilot hidden, state-coupled exact outputs with an early design blind; external benchmarks support the direction, but local evidence rules out relying on small stated-rule traps or low scores without QC.
+
+## 1. What outside benchmarks make hard and fair
+
+**Finding.** The 19-source ledger in `SOURCES.md` shows four transferable patterns: fresh held-out instances, long state transitions, exact or externally checked outcomes, and repeated attempts to measure reliability. Terminal-Bench's own authors stress manually validating tests and specified properties ([Terminal-Bench paper, 17 Jan 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.11868)); τ-bench uses stateful policies and pass^k ([18 Jun 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12045)); MLE-bench scores unseen competition outcomes ([9 Oct 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.07095)); OSWorld 2.0 identifies cross-source and implicit-state failures ([28 Jun 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.29537)). Freshness is a contamination control, not proof of difficulty: SWE-bench-Live refreshes issues ([29 May 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.23419)), while OpenAI later found defects in established SWE benchmarks ([Verified audit, 23 Feb 2026](https://openai.com/index/why-we-no-longer-evaluate-swe-bench-verified/); [Pro audit, 8 Jul 2026](https://openai.com/index/separating-signal-from-noise-coding-evaluations/)). These are different benchmark settings; transfer to our Opus pipeline is **INFERRED**.
+
+**What to change.** In `master_chat_tasks/prompts/templates/P2*` and the design gate of `knowledge/LEVER_RULES_v3.md`, require a compact outside-analogy note, a hidden-instance generator, an independent oracle, a first-program wrong-output witness, and a task-level contract review before a full build. Keep the existing rule trace and QC authority (`LEVER_RULES_v3.md:29-37`; `Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:19-21,35-38`). **Expected gain, INFERRED:** fewer attractive but invalid or solver-easy ideas entering full build. **Could break:** an overstrict early screen may discard a hard task whose first blind solver happens to be unusually good. **Check:** preserve rejected pilots and compare the next two ideas' blind scores and QC findings against the current process; no causal gain is yet measured (`S5_build_method/FINDINGS.md:9,48`).
+
+## 2. Which ideas are new, used, or failed here
+
+**Finding.** Already used or tested: hidden reruns and heavy hidden weight (`knowledge/ANGLES.md:8-10`), exact whole outputs and process probes (`knowledge/LEVER_RULES_v3.md:65-80,96-113`), bounded induction and counterfactual figures (`:103-109`), and a three-blind/generic-solver gate (`:230-244`). The local 30-task low-score sample attributes 30.80% of measured lost final points to held-out/scenario exact checks and 26.62% to trajectory criteria; the labels are **INFERRED** from check names and targets (`S4_task_study/FINDINGS.md:11-28`). The 90-task angle log found rerunning common in every difficulty band, so rerun itself was not separating (`knowledge/ANGLES.md:9-10`).
+
+Failed locally: explicit small contracts, replay-only tasks, and stated algorithm limits often gave a fresh blind the exact method (`LEVER_RULES_v3.md:18-25,77-84`; `S5_build_method/FINDINGS.md:5-7`). Registry query `jq -r '.tasks[] | select(.pipeline.stage=="parked") | [.slug,.pipeline.next] | @tsv' master_chat_tasks/registry/tasks.json` returns 11 parked tasks: cleanroom says `Blind 3/3 at 100 (98/98)`, crash recovery `3/3 ... (95/95)`, batch lease `3/3 at 139/139`, config compiler `19/19 right`, and multicorner `tests 100 / judge 30.77`. Some registry entries reflect old parking decisions; current policy says only Ayush parks (`LEVER_RULES_v3.md:229-231`). The current gate remains tests <85, rubric <85, final <40 (`LEVER_RULES_v3.md:224-228`).
+
+**New-to-us candidates, INFERRED:** (a) measuring *pass across seeds* as a reliability curve, inspired by τ-bench pass^k ([18 Jun 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12045)); (b) a recorded “first wrong state transition” and recovery annotation, inspired by METR's observation that agents fail to react to new information ([22 Nov 2024](https://metr.org/blog/2024-11-22-evaluating-r-d-capabilities-of-llms/)); (c) diagnostic judge perturbations for order and model-family bias, supported by [CodeJudgeBench, 14 Jul 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.10535), [rubric position study, 2 Feb 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02219), and [self-preference study, 8 Apr 2026](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.06996). These are method improvements rather than proven hardness levers. **What to change:** add them as diagnostic fields in `master_chat_tasks/knowledge/ANGLES.md` and run records, not as scored tests. **Expected gain, INFERRED:** distinguish a real generalized miss from seed luck and judge noise. **Could break:** extra annotation cost and false confidence from small samples. **Check:** compare at least three seeded outputs and judge decisions on two frozen runs; report disagreement and retain raw evidence.
+
+## 3. Which task ideas have a plausible wrong first program
+
+**Finding.** `IDEAS.md` ranks ten task concepts with domain, deliverable, wrong-first-program mechanism, legal contract, blind-kill condition and subjective chance/cost. The leading pilots are rolling reservations, coupled network repair and backdated entitlement replay (`IDEAS.md:5-13`). Their hypothesized loss is a valid but wrong output on hidden data: state aggregation, greedy shared-capacity optimization, or forward-only correction respectively. This matches the local requirement that a lever cause WRONG output, except a narrowly bounded hidden-scale allowance (`LEVER_RULES_v3.md:85-91`). It also avoids the parked pattern of replay with a fully explicit small contract (`S5_build_method/FINDINGS.md:5-7`). The chance percentages are **INFERRED** and **UNVERIFIED**.
+
+**What to change.** Feed rank 1 to a design-only P2 pilot: draft contract, small shipped sample, independent oracle, three hidden seeds, and a strong generic baseline before any full task authoring (`IDEAS.md:21-26`; `S5_build_method/FINDINGS.md:9`). **Expected gain, INFERRED:** spend build time only when at least two blind programs return wrong documents on valid hidden inputs. **Could break:** generator or oracle mistakes may masquerade as agent failure; some state ideas are already locally easy. **Check:** prove every graded rule is stated or determined (`05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:19-21`), trigger each ordering rule (`:38`), cross-check the oracle on small exhaustive cases, then run the design blind. Reject if a blind or generic solver is exact on all three hidden seeds.
+
+## 4. How each paid run should teach us more
+
+**Finding.** The existing local workflow already calls for Phase A byte/profile comparison before diagnosis and one valid paid run on frozen QC-passed bytes (`LEVER_RULES_v3.md:228-244`). The S4 study warns that check-name loss categories are **INFERRED**, and timeout can hide behind an exact-output check (`S4_task_study/FINDINGS.md:11-26`). METR publishes multiple attempts and time-horizon reliability ([updated 8 Sep 2026](https://metr.org/time-horizons/)), and its RE-Bench analysis describes failures to update a plan after new information ([22 Nov 2024](https://metr.org/blog/2024-11-22-evaluating-r-d-capabilities-of-llms/)). CodeJudgeBench finds judge randomness and order sensitivity ([14 Jul 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.10535)).
+
+**What to change.** Add one `RUN_LEARNING` card to each frozen run's read-out: (1) exact tree/profile and test IDs; (2) first wrong assumption with trajectory step and the first separating input; (3) whether the agent detected the failure and revised graded logic; (4) per-check WRONG/TIMEOUT/CRASH/INVALID/ORACLE-ISSUE, with raw output and runtime evidence; (5) seed pass vector and pass-all-k for the available seeds; (6) judge criterion evidence and a diagnostic order/wording perturbation on a copy; (7) one counterfactual: would the same program pass if the separating event were removed? The card is a proposed method, **INFERRED** from sources 7, 12, 13, 16-18 in `SOURCES.md`, and extends the current TRIP/fix receipt recommendation (`S4_task_study/FINDINGS.md:36-44`; `S5_build_method/FINDINGS.md:50-52`). **Expected gain, INFERRED:** separate causal failure mechanisms from names, score variance and evaluator defects. **Could break:** extra read-out time; perturbing the official rubric would invalidate score comparison. **Check:** apply retrospectively to one high and one low existing run using copies; ask whether the card changes the stated lever diagnosis while the official score stays untouched.
+
+## Limits
+
+No task, code, test or running work was changed. Source ledger includes three pages whose exact publication date was not verified (`SOURCES.md:17-23`); all proposed score chances and gains are **INFERRED/UNVERIFIED** until a design blind and valid frozen run.
